---
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import {
	PLATFORM_ZONES,
	PLATFORM_STAGES,
	PLATFORM_FOUNDATIONS,
	PLATFORM_RUNS,
	PLATFORM_CORE,
	type Zone,
	type PlatformRun,
} from '@data/platform-visual';
import mark from '@root/assets/images/landings/draft/thingsboard-mark.svg?raw';

/**
 * The platform overview, taken from `PlatformRaised` to work on one number: what the drawing's
 * smallest type measures between the stacking threshold and the unit cap.
 *
 * AT THE MOMENT THIS IS A VERBATIM COPY of `PlatformRaised`. It is here so the direction has
 * somewhere to go without touching the visual the homepage renders, per the rule in this
 * directory's CLAUDE.md. Every difference from the incumbent will be recorded below as it is made;
 * while this list is empty, the two are the same drawing and the sandbox is showing a pair of
 * identical exhibits on purpose.
 *
 * WHAT IT IS FOR. The wide form is NATIVE 1128 units with a floor of u(14), so R is 80.6 — against
 * 52.5 for `DigitalTwin` and 28 for the compact family, the two ends of the range recorded in
 * CLAUDE.md. R is the whole story between 768 and 1297 container px, because the rendered floor is
 * exactly `container ÷ R`: it is 9.5px where the wide form starts and does not reach 14px until the
 * container does 1128. The narrow form below 768 is not the problem — NARROW 340 against the same
 * floor is R 24.3, and the note on that constant records the move that got it there, which was to
 * make the drawing smaller rather than the type bigger.
 *
 * WHAT DIFFERS FROM `PlatformRaised` SO FAR:
 *
 * - NO ZONE EMBLEMS. The chip and the people glyph are gone from the two zone headers. They sat
 *   beside the zone title at 26 units plus a 10-unit gap, and the title is the one line in that
 *   card that was short of room — "Your people and operations" needed 190 units in a 182-unit box.
 *   Taking the emblem out gives the title the header's full 218 and costs the card nothing, since
 *   the column's width is set by the item rows below, not by the header.
 * - THE SIDE COLUMNS ARE WIDER AND SHORTER. 280 units rather than 248, and their own row rather
 *   than spanning the two band rows, so they hug their three items instead of being stretched to
 *   the platform's height. The width buys the slack the overlap needs; the height is what turns the
 *   runs into U-shapes, since the bands above and below are now clear of all three cards.
 * - THE PLATFORM OVERLAPS THEM by 24 units a side, paid for as a negative margin on the card
 *   rather than as a negative grid track, which is not a thing. Its shadow falls across their inner
 *   edge, which is the reading that makes it the raised object.
 * - A RULE UNDER THE HEADING, separating "ThingsBoard Platform" from the four stages. The card's
 *   own hairline, the one the foundations strip already uses.
 * - NARROWER, at 1050 units rather than 1128. The gutters come down from 36 to 12 and the middle
 *   from 560 to 530 — the core's side padding from 26 to 20, and the stage grid's column gap from
 *   18 to 12. The side columns are untouched: 180 units is the measured width of "Gateways and
 *   platforms" on one line and the column is exactly that plus 68 of chrome, so there is nothing
 *   there to take without breaking the string.
 *
 * OVERLAP WAS TRIED ON PAPER AND IS NOT AVAILABLE. Running the core over the zone cards' inner
 * edge would narrow the composition further, but those cards carry 220 units of content in 220
 * units of space — the overlap would land on the item text rather than on air. It needs shorter
 * strings first, which is a copy decision.
 *
 * A CANDIDATE BESIDE `PlatformRaised`, not a replacement for it. The two are separate files on
 * purpose — the same reason `ConnectCloud` and `ConnectFlow` are, and `DeployFork` restates the
 * connect visual's vocabulary rather than importing it: a rejected direction has to be able to die
 * without taking the other one with it. The path arithmetic below is therefore deliberately a copy,
 * and the numbers it is fed are this version's own.
 *
 * What differs from `PlatformLoop`, all of it in the middle column except the first:
 *
 * - LOOSER. `PlatformLoop` was tightened until the composition was 316 units tall, which turned
 *   out to be a few units past comfortable. The band is 34 rather than 30 and the paddings inside
 *   all three cards come back up; the inline heads, which is where most of the tightening actually
 *   came from, stay inline.
 * - RAISED. The platform carries the page's ambient shadow and no border. A hairline as well as a
 *   shadow reads as a card with an outline drawn on it; the shadow alone is what makes it sit above
 *   the two zones, which is the whole point of the treatment.
 * - NO RAIL. The arrow through the four stages is gone. It said the four were a sequence, which is
 *   a claim the four verbs already make on their own, and it was competing with the four runs
 *   around the outside — which are the arrows that carry the argument. Removing it also removes the
 *   knockout machinery it needed: the stage icons no longer paint the surface behind themselves or
 *   carry a gap on the right, because there is no longer a line for them to break.
 * - THE TYPE IN THE MIDDLE IS INK. The four verbs and the heading are both near-black; the colour
 *   in that column is the badge and the four stage icons and nothing else.
 * - THE MARK IS A BADGE, straddling the card's top edge the way the ecosystem cards place a product
 *   mark, at one weight below it rather than a lockup in two voices.
 * - THE FOUR STAGES ARE CODED AS THE FOUR SECTIONS. Each takes its section's colour, by reference
 *   from `platform-visual.ts`, so the column is a legend for the rest of the page; the icons stay
 *   the stage's own. The two outer columns follow from it: equipment takes the first stage's hue
 *   and people the last, being the stages they meet.
 *
 * - IT STAYS HORIZONTAL AND SCALES. Four stages across, at every width down to 768, where the whole
 *   composition goes vertical for phones and small tablets. That is where this cut parts company
 *   with `PlatformLoop`'s numbers, which stack at 1000.
 *
 *   The cost is R, and it is worth knowing rather than finding later. R is `NATIVE / floor` and the
 *   rendered size of the smallest type is exactly `container / R`. This is 1128 units on a 14-unit
 *   floor, so R is 80.6: 15.3px at its own width, 11.9px in the 960 column of the centred section it
 *   is headed for, 9.8px at 768 just before it turns. Two rows of two was tried and would have put
 *   that middle number at 14.5px — but it turns the middle into a block of four tiles instead of the
 *   run the drawing is about, so the 2.6px is being paid on purpose.
 *
 *   What is NOT being paid: 1128 is the composition tightened as far as it goes with four across —
 *   gutters 44 to 36, card chrome 76 to 68, and the floor raised from 13.5 units to 14, which is the
 *   most the side column's 180-unit measure will take without a note wrapping.
 *
 * Everything else — the palette, the run geometry, what the composition is saying — is as
 * `PlatformLoop` documents it, and that file is the one to read for why. Its numbers are its own:
 * it is still 1160 units at R 86 and still stacks at 1000.
 */

interface Props {
	/** Show the foundations strip under the four stages. Off by default; see `PlatformLoop`. */
	foundations?: boolean;
}

const { foundations = false } = Astro.props;

// --- the grid, in design units -------------------------------------------------------------

// The side columns are as narrow as the copy allows and no narrower. Measured: "Gateways and
// platforms" needs 179.6 units on one line, and a card spends 73 on chrome (17 of padding a side,
// a 1px border, a 25-unit icon and the 12 beside it) — so 253 is the floor, and 256 clears it by
// three. A name that wraps is not a cosmetic problem here: the three items divide the card's height
// equally, so one taller row in the left column would put it out of step with the right, which is
// the alignment this composition is built on.
//
// FOUR ACROSS, and the middle keeps its 560 for them. The picture is a left-to-right loop and the
// four stages are the middle of it; stacking them two by two made the middle a block rather than a
// run, which is not what the drawing says.
//
// 248 rather than 256, and 36 of gutter rather than 44 — the two places there was air to take. The
// card's own chrome came down from 76 units to 68 (17 of padding a side to 14, and 12 between icon
// and text to 10), and 180 is the measured width of "Gateways and platforms" on one line, which is
// the number the side column cannot go under.
const COL = { side: 264, gut: 0, mid: 482 };
/** How far the platform runs over each side column. */
const OVERLAP = 12;
const NATIVE = COL.side * 2 + COL.gut * 2 + COL.mid; // 1010
// 340, not 480. Measured at a 335px phone column, 480 units put the composition's floor at 9.8px
// and left every card 230 units wider than the longest thing in it, so the text hugged the left edge
// of a mostly empty box. 340 is what the CORE needs and nothing more: 288 of content after its
// padding, which gives the two stage columns 135 each and holds "Rule chains, calculated fields,
// alarms" in two lines. The same column now renders the drawing at 340 x 0.985, so the floor is
// 13.8px — the type grew by 41% by making the drawing smaller.
const NARROW = 340;

// --- the four runs -------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** 34 rather than `PlatformLoop`'s 30: the loosening starts here, where the picture breathes most. */
const BAND = 34;
const LANE = 6;
const BEND = 10;
const HEAD = 6;
const WING = 4;

const TURN = 112;
const MID_C = COL.gut + COL.mid / 2;
const IN_X = MID_C - TURN;

/**
 * The horizontal part of a run: from the card edge it leaves to the column where it turns.
 *
 * Each run label is a box of exactly this width, so centring the text inside it centres the text on
 * the arrow — and a label that is later made longer or shorter stays centred, because the box never
 * moves. The two sides are the same number because `TURN` is symmetric about the middle.
 */
/** The stacked form's label box. The wide cut sizes its labels from `RUN_W` instead. */
const LEG = IN_X;

/**
 * The app badge in the middle of the platform: the page's shared squircle, as the connect section
 * draws it — 32% radius, the mark in white on the brand, and the same 69% mark-to-tile it settled
 * on, which is what `PAD` below is for.
 */
const BADGE = 76;
const BADGE_PAD = Math.round((BADGE * (1 - 0.69)) / 2); // 10 -> a 44-unit mark in a 64-unit tile

/**
 * The equipment column is coloured as Connect and the people column as Operate — the stage each of
 * them actually meets, since the loop enters the platform at the first stage and leaves it at the
 * last. Read off `PLATFORM_STAGES` by position rather than restated here, so the pair cannot drift
 * from the icons it is meant to match.
 */
const ZONE_IN = PLATFORM_STAGES[0].accent;
const ZONE_OUT = PLATFORM_STAGES[PLATFORM_STAGES.length - 1].accent;

/**
 * Stacked, the distance from the card's centre to each run's stem.
 *
 * The two lanes used to sit at the far edges of the card — measured 291px apart at the 960 probe —
 * which read as two rails down the sides with a hole between them rather than as two channels
 * joining one card to the next. At 56 they flank the middle, and the labels go OUTBOARD of them so
 * the two never meet in the centre.
 *
 * 56 is also the floor: the badge straddling the platform's top edge is 64 wide, so it reaches 32
 * either side of the same centre line, and a stem at 56 clears its edge by 17.
 */
const LANE_X = 56;

/** One sweep for all four, because the loop goes one way. */
const CW = 1;
const LO = BAND - LANE;

/**
 * The U-shaped runs.
 *
 * A run no longer leaves a card's inner edge — the platform overlaps that edge now. It leaves the
 * TOP or BOTTOM of its card instead, turns across the band, and comes back down (or up) onto the
 * platform. Every endpoint therefore sits on a row boundary the band SVG can address: the cards are
 * `align-self: start`, so all three top edges are the band's bottom edge, and the platform's bottom
 * edge is the lower band's top. The one distance that is NOT computable here is the gap between a
 * side card's bottom and the row's bottom, since the card hugs its own text — `.plat__drop` covers
 * that in CSS, where it is just the free space in a flex column.
 */
const ATTACH_L = COL.side / 2;
const ATTACH_R = NATIVE - ATTACH_L;
const CORE_IN = NATIVE / 2 - TURN;
const CORE_OUT = NATIVE / 2 + TURN;
/** A label is as wide as its run's horizontal reach, so centring the text centres it on the arrow. */
const RUN_W = CORE_IN - ATTACH_L;

/**
 * The signal, as `ConnectHub` and `NormalizeSeries` draw theirs: a dashed copy of the wire whose
 * offset animates one full pattern per cycle, so a single lit segment travels the path.
 *
 * It carries the DIRECTION, which is why it is worth the motion — a path is drawn from its source to
 * its destination, so the pip runs the way the arrow points and the pair of runs on each side read
 * as a loop rather than as two lines.
 *
 * `PATTERN` is longer than the longest path (about 310 units), so exactly one pip is on a wire at a
 * time and there is a dark beat between them rather than a chain of dashes.
 */
const CYCLE_MS = 1800;
const PIP_DASH = 14;
const PIP_PATTERN = 380;
/** Golden-ratio phases, so four runs never pulse together and the loop has no beat of its own. */
const pipPhase = (i: number) => +(((i * 0.618033988749895) % 1) * CYCLE_MS).toFixed(1);

const TOP = {
	telemetry: {
		line: `M ${ATTACH_L},${BAND} V ${LANE + BEND} A ${BEND},${BEND} 0 0 ${CW} ${ATTACH_L + BEND},${LANE} H ${CORE_IN - BEND} A ${BEND},${BEND} 0 0 ${CW} ${CORE_IN},${LANE + BEND} V ${BAND}`,
		head: `M ${CORE_IN - WING},${BAND - HEAD} L ${CORE_IN},${BAND} L ${CORE_IN + WING},${BAND - HEAD}`,
	},
	views: {
		line: `M ${CORE_OUT},${BAND} V ${LANE + BEND} A ${BEND},${BEND} 0 0 ${CW} ${CORE_OUT + BEND},${LANE} H ${ATTACH_R - BEND} A ${BEND},${BEND} 0 0 ${CW} ${ATTACH_R},${LANE + BEND} V ${BAND}`,
		// No head here. This run ENDS on the right card, whose top edge is not the band's bottom any
		// more — the cards are centred on the platform's axis now, so the last stretch is the CSS
		// tail and the head belongs at its far end, against the card.
		head: '',
	},
};

const BOT = {
	commands: {
		line: `M ${CORE_IN},0 V ${LO - BEND} A ${BEND},${BEND} 0 0 ${CW} ${CORE_IN - BEND},${LO} H ${ATTACH_L + BEND} A ${BEND},${BEND} 0 0 ${CW} ${ATTACH_L},${LO - BEND} V 0`,
		// No head here, for the same reason: this run ends on the LEFT card, and its head rides the
		// tail below the band. Leaving it here put an arrowhead in the middle of a line.
		head: '',
	},
	actions: {
		line: `M ${ATTACH_R},0 V ${LO - BEND} A ${BEND},${BEND} 0 0 ${CW} ${ATTACH_R - BEND},${LO} H ${CORE_OUT + BEND} A ${BEND},${BEND} 0 0 ${CW} ${CORE_OUT},${LO - BEND} V 0`,
		head: `M ${CORE_OUT - WING},${HEAD} L ${CORE_OUT},0 L ${CORE_OUT + WING},${HEAD}`,
	},
};

const BANDS = { in: [TOP.telemetry, TOP.views], out: [BOT.commands, BOT.actions] };

// --- the stacked form's arrows ---------------------------------------------------------------

const STEM = { w: 14, h: 12 };
const HEAD_D = `M ${STEM.w / 2 - WING},${STEM.h - HEAD} L ${STEM.w / 2},${STEM.h} L ${STEM.w / 2 + WING},${STEM.h - HEAD}`;

/**
 * The arrowhead's BOX, which is taller than its path.
 *
 * The chevron's point sits at `STEM.h`, the bottom of the viewBox — and a 3-wide stroke with a round
 * join paints 1.5 further out than the geometry it follows. An `svg` clips to its viewBox by default,
 * so the point was being sliced flat: every arrowhead in this drawing ended in a chopped stub rather
 * than a point. Measured, not guessed — the painted box came back exactly the path's 8x6 with the
 * whole overhang gone.
 *
 * So the box carries half a stroke of room at the bottom. The path is unchanged, the point still
 * lands on the card's edge, and the stroke that draws it now has somewhere to go.
 */
const HEAD_STROKE = 3;
const HEAD_BOX_H = STEM.h + HEAD_STROKE / 2; // 13.5

const ORDER = ['equipment', 'platform', 'people'];
const dirOf = (r: PlatformRun) => (ORDER.indexOf(r.from) < ORDER.indexOf(r.to) ? 'down' : 'up');

/**
 * The zone a run belongs to — the end of it that is not the platform.
 *
 * NOT `from`: two of the four runs leave the platform, so keying colour off the source tags the
 * outbound half of each pair as `platform` and leaves it grey. A run belongs to the side it touches,
 * which for `commands` (platform to equipment) is the LEFT card even though it starts in the middle.
 */
const zoneOf = (id: PlatformRun['id']) => {
	const r = PLATFORM_RUNS.find((x) => x.id === id)!;
	return r.from === 'platform' ? r.to : r.from;
};
const bandSrc = { in: ['telemetry', 'views'], out: ['commands', 'actions'] } as const;

const zoneItems = (z: Zone) => z.items;

const vars = [
	`--plat-u: min(1.15px, calc(100cqw / ${NATIVE}))`,
	`--plat-u-narrow: min(1px, calc(100cqw / ${NARROW}))`,
	`--plat-native: ${NATIVE}`,
	`--plat-narrow: ${NARROW}`,
	`--plat-col-side: ${COL.side}`,
	`--plat-col-gut: ${COL.gut}`,
	`--plat-col-mid: ${COL.mid}`,
	`--plat-overlap: ${OVERLAP}`,
	`--plat-attach: ${ATTACH_L}`,
	`--plat-core-out: ${CORE_OUT}`,
	`--plat-run-w: ${RUN_W}`,
	`--plat-pip-dash: ${PIP_DASH} ${PIP_PATTERN - PIP_DASH}`,
	`--plat-pip-pattern: ${PIP_PATTERN}`,
	`--plat-cycle: ${CYCLE_MS}ms`,
	...PLATFORM_STAGES.map((st, i) => `--plat-stage-${i + 1}: ${st.accent}`),
	`--plat-head-h: ${HEAD_BOX_H}`,
	`--plat-band: ${BAND}`,
	`--plat-lane: ${LANE_X}`,
	`--plat-leg: ${LEG}`,
	`--plat-badge: ${BADGE}`,
	`--plat-badge-pad: ${BADGE_PAD}`,
	`--plat-equipment: ${ZONE_IN}`,
	`--plat-people: ${ZONE_OUT}`,
].join('; ');
---

<div class="plat-fit">
	<div class="plat" style={vars} data-found={foundations ? '' : undefined}>
		{
			PLATFORM_ZONES.map((z) => (
				<div class={`plat__col plat__col--${z.id}`}>
					<span class="plat__tail plat__tail--lift" aria-hidden="true">
						<span class="plat__tail-line" />
						{z.id === 'people' && (
							<svg class="plat__tail-head" viewBox={`0 0 ${STEM.w} ${HEAD_BOX_H}`}>
								<path d={HEAD_D} />
							</svg>
						)}
					</span>
					<section class={`plat__zone plat__zone--${z.id}`} aria-labelledby={`plat-r-${z.id}`}>
						<header class="plat__zone-head">
							{/* `<p>`, not `<h3>`. These name their zone for `aria-labelledby` above, and that
						    works against any element — but as headings they also entered the document
						    outline, so a heading-by-heading read of the page went "…all-in-one platform →
						    Your people and operations → Your equipment and data → ThingsBoard Platform →
						    Connect any IoT devices", three 12.8px diagram fragments sitting at the same
						    level as the five feature rows. The zones stay reachable as named regions,
						    which is the mechanism that actually suits a diagram. */}
							<p class="plat__zone-title" id={`plat-r-${z.id}`}>
								{z.title}
							</p>
						</header>
						<ul class="plat__items">
							{zoneItems(z).map((it) => (
								<li class="plat__item">
									<Icon class="plat__item-icon" name={it.icon} aria-hidden="true" />
									<div>
										<p class="plat__item-name">{it.name}</p>
										<p class="plat__item-note">{it.note}</p>
									</div>
								</li>
							))}
						</ul>
					</section>
					{/* The vertical half of the lower U. The band below can address every other endpoint
				    in units, but not this one: the card hugs its text, so where its bottom edge lands
				    is a runtime fact. As the free space in a flex column it needs no number at all. */}
					<span class="plat__tail plat__tail--drop" aria-hidden="true">
						{z.id === 'equipment' && (
							<svg class="plat__tail-head plat__tail-head--up" viewBox={`0 0 ${STEM.w} ${HEAD_BOX_H}`}>
								<path d={HEAD_D} />
							</svg>
						)}
						<span class="plat__tail-line" />
					</span>
				</div>
			))
		}

		{
			PLATFORM_RUNS.map((r) => (
				<p class={`plat__run plat__run--${r.id}`} data-zone={zoneOf(r.id)}>
					<span class="plat__stem" data-dir={dirOf(r)} aria-hidden="true">
						<span class="plat__stem-line" />
						<svg class="plat__stem-head" viewBox={`0 0 ${STEM.w} ${HEAD_BOX_H}`}>
							<path d={HEAD_D} />
						</svg>
					</span>
					<span class="plat__run-label">{r.label}</span>
				</p>
			))
		}

		{
			/* Before the platform in the DOM, so the card paints over the last unit of each run and a
		    rounded join cannot show a seam at the edge. The card's shadow falls across those same
		    units, which is the right reading: the runs pass under something raised. */
		}
		{
			(['in', 'out'] as const).map((which) => (
				<div class={`plat__band plat__band--${which}`} aria-hidden="true">
					<svg class="plat__wires" viewBox={`0 0 ${NATIVE} ${BAND}`} preserveAspectRatio="none">
						{BANDS[which].map((r, i) => (
							<Fragment>
								<path class="plat__wire" data-zone={zoneOf(bandSrc[which][i])} d={r.line} />
								<path
									class="plat__pip"
									data-zone={zoneOf(bandSrc[which][i])}
									d={r.line}
									style={`--pip-phase: ${pipPhase(which === 'in' ? i : i + 2)}ms`}
								/>
								{r.head && <path class="plat__head" data-zone={zoneOf(bandSrc[which][i])} d={r.head} />}
							</Fragment>
						))}
					</svg>
				</div>
			))
		}

		<section class="plat__core" aria-labelledby="plat-r-core">
			{
				/* Straddling the card's top edge rather than sitting inside it — the placement the
			    ecosystem cards and the deployment visual give a product mark. Out of flow, so the
			    heading's position does not depend on how big the badge is. */
			}
			<span class="plat__core-badge" aria-hidden="true" set:html={mark} />
			{
				/* `<p>` for the same reason as the zone titles above: it names this region for
			    `aria-labelledby`, which does not need it to be a heading. */
			}
			<p class="plat__core-title" id="plat-r-core">{PLATFORM_CORE.brand} {PLATFORM_CORE.product}</p>

			{
				/* No rail, so no absolute positioning and no knockout: a stage is just an icon, a verb
			    and a line of detail, in that order and in normal flow. */
			}
			<ol class="plat__stages">
				{
					PLATFORM_STAGES.map((s) => (
						<li class="plat__stage" style={`--stage: ${s.accent}`}>
							<Icon class="plat__stage-icon" name={s.icon} aria-hidden="true" />
							<p class="plat__stage-name">{s.name}</p>
							<p class="plat__stage-note">{s.note}</p>
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ol>

			<ul class="plat__found">
				{
					PLATFORM_FOUNDATIONS.map((f) => (
						<li class="plat__found-item">
							<Icon class="plat__found-icon" name={f.icon} aria-hidden="true" />
							<span>{f.label}</span>
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ul>
		</section>
	</div>
</div>

<style lang="scss">
	@use '../../styles/variables' as *;

	@function u($n) {
		@return calc(#{$n} * var(--u));
	}

	.plat-fit {
		container-type: inline-size;
	}

	.plat {
		--u: var(--plat-u);

		// `--plat-equipment` and `--plat-people` are published by the frontmatter, off the first and
		// last stage's accent. They are deliberately NOT restated here: a fallback would be a second
		// copy of the pairing, free to go stale the moment a stage's hue changes.
		--plat-platform: #{$color-brand};

		// White, like the two zones. The platform is told apart by being LIFTED, not by being tinted —
		// a shadow and a fill were two answers to the same question, and the tint was also the last
		// thing making the middle column a coloured area rather than a white card with coloured marks
		// on it.
		--plat-paper: var(--color-bg-surface, #fff);
		--plat-surface: var(--plat-paper);
		// One grey for every part of a run: the wire, its head, and the label that names it. Colour
		// has moved off the runs and onto the icons, so a run is a connector and nothing else.
		--plat-wire: var(--color-text-secondary, #63656c);
		// A run belongs to the zone it touches, so it takes that zone's hue rather than the drawing's
		// grey: the pair on the left is Connect's accent — the first stage, which is the same colour
		// the equipment zone already titles itself in — and the pair on the right is the brand.
		// `data-zone` is on every wire, head and label for exactly this.
		--plat-wire-in: var(--plat-equipment);
		--plat-wire-out: var(--color-primary, #{$color-brand});
		--plat-line: var(--color-border, #ebebeb);
		--plat-ink: var(--color-text, #17181c);
		--plat-muted: var(--color-text-secondary, #5b616e);

		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns:
			u(var(--plat-col-side)) u(var(--plat-col-gut)) u(var(--plat-col-mid))
			u(var(--plat-col-gut)) u(var(--plat-col-side));
		grid-template-rows: auto u(var(--plat-band)) auto u(var(--plat-band)) auto;
		max-width: u(var(--plat-native));
		margin: 0 auto;
	}

	// --- the two outer zones ---------------------------------------------------------------

	// The grid cell. Row 3 only, where the card used to span 2/5: the bands are now clear space
	// above and below all three cards rather than a channel between two tall ones, which is what
	// the U-shaped runs need. The card hugs its text at the top of this column and the drop line
	// takes whatever is left, so neither has to know the other's height.
	.plat__col {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		grid-row: 3;
		// The card sits on the platform's own horizontal axis: the tails above and below take an
		// equal share of whatever is left, so the card centres itself without either of them — or
		// the run geometry — knowing how tall it is.
		justify-content: center;
	}

	.plat__col--equipment {
		grid-column: 1;
	}

	.plat__col--people {
		grid-column: 5;
	}

	// The vertical run between a card and its band. Drawn in CSS because its length is the column's
	// free space rather than a number the frontmatter can work out — and `flex: 1 1 0` on both is
	// what centres the card between them.
	.plat__tail {
		display: flex;
		flex: 1 1 0;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: center;
		// The head's box is 13.5 units and the gap between card and band is whatever is left over —
		// which, once the items got their air back, was four. An arrowhead cannot fit in a gap
		// shorter than itself, so the tail states a floor and the row grows to it instead.
		min-height: u(18);
	}

	// 3 units, matching the wires it continues.
	.plat__tail-line {
		flex: 1 1 auto;
		width: u(3);
		border-radius: u(1.5);
		background: var(--plat-wire);
	}

	// Same chevron the stacked form's stems use, and the same box trick: see `HEAD_BOX_H`. It rides
	// the tail's card end, so the point meets the card rather than the row boundary.
	.plat__tail-head {
		flex: none;
		// Over the line whatever the DOM order is, so the line can run under the chevron from either
		// side without its rounded cap showing past the point.
		position: relative;
		z-index: 1;
		width: u(14);
		height: u(var(--plat-head-h));
		max-width: none;
		// 12, not 6. At 6 the two inks met exactly — and "exactly" is the arms' OPEN ends, so the
		// line stopped where the V starts and left a triangular void either side of it, which reads
		// as an arrowhead floating off the end of its line. The chevron's ink is 6 units deep, so a
		// further 6 runs the line all the way to the apex and the arms sit on it.
		margin-top: u(-12);
		fill: none;
		stroke: var(--plat-wire);
		stroke-width: 3;
		stroke-linecap: round;
		stroke-linejoin: round;
	}

	.plat__tail-head--up {
		margin: 0 0 u(-12);
		transform: scaleY(-1);
	}

	.plat__zone {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		// Asymmetric on purpose: the edge the platform runs over carries the overlap as padding, so
		// the card's CONTENT — the item rows and the hairlines between them — stops before the
		// platform starts. Without it those hairlines ran on under the card and reappeared past its
		// rounded corner, which read as stray lines beside the drawing.
		padding: u(16) u(14);
		padding-inline-end: calc(#{u(14)} + #{u(var(--plat-overlap))});
		border: max(1px, #{u(1)}) solid var(--plat-line);
		border-radius: u(10);
		background: var(--plat-paper);
	}

	// NO BORDER ON THE EDGE THE PLATFORM COVERS.
	//
	// The paint order was never wrong — measured at eighteen points across both overlap bands, the
	// core is the top element every time. What read as the side column being in front was this: both
	// cards are white, the platform deliberately carries no outline (a hairline as well as a shadow
	// reads as a card with an outline drawn on it), and the zone card's hairline ran on underneath it
	// and died there. So the only edge visible along the seam belonged to the LOWER card, and the eye
	// believes the edge it can see. Ending that border where the platform begins lets the zone tuck
	// under instead of appearing to cross in front. The rounded corner on that side is 10 units and
	// the overlap is 12, so the corner is covered and nothing is left half-drawn.
	.plat__zone--equipment {
		border-right: 0;
	}

	.plat__zone--people {
		border-left: 0;
	}

	.plat__zone--equipment {
		--zone: var(--plat-equipment);
	}

	.plat__zone--people {
		--zone: var(--plat-people);
		// Mirrored: the platform overlaps this card's LEFT edge.
		padding-inline: calc(#{u(14)} + #{u(var(--plat-overlap))}) u(14);
	}

	// A FIXED HEIGHT, sized for a title on two lines (2 x 15 at 1.2, plus the 14 below it).
	//
	// The two titles are not the same length, and at 256-unit columns "Your people and operations"
	// needs 190.6 units for one line and has 184 — so it wraps and the equipment title does not.
	// Without this the two heads differ by exactly that line, and since the items below divide
	// whatever height is left, every row in the left column sits above its opposite number: measured
	// 10, 6.7 and 3.3 units of drift down the card. Pinning the head means the two cannot disagree,
	// whichever title wraps and whatever the copy is changed to later.
	.plat__zone-head {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		// 32, not 52. The 52 was sized around a 26-unit emblem that is no longer there, so the
		// heading was sitting in half a row of its own air.
		min-height: u(32);
		gap: u(10);
		padding-bottom: u(10);
		border-bottom: max(1px, #{u(1)}) solid var(--plat-line);
	}

	.plat__zone-title {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: u(15);
		font-weight: $font-weight-medium;
		line-height: 1.2;
		letter-spacing: -0.005em;
		color: var(--zone);
	}

	.plat__items {
		display: flex;
		flex: 1 1 auto;
		flex-direction: column;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
		list-style: none;
	}

	// Equal shares no longer distribute anything — the card hugs its content now, so there is no
	// spare height to share out and each row is its own padding plus its text. `flex: 1 1 0` stays
	// for the narrow cut, where the card is still stretched. 10 rather than 14: the padding is the
	// only thing between the rows and the card's height, and the card is meant to sit shorter than
	// the platform it is beside.
	.plat__item {
		display: flex;
		flex: 1 1 0;
		align-items: center;
		gap: u(10);
		padding: u(10) 0;

		& + & {
			border-top: max(1px, #{u(1)}) solid var(--plat-line);
		}
	}

	.plat__item-icon {
		flex: none;
		width: u(25);
		height: u(25);
		color: var(--zone);
	}

	.plat__item-name {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: u(16);
		font-weight: $font-weight-bold;
		line-height: 1.2;
		letter-spacing: -0.015em;
		color: var(--plat-ink);
	}

	/* The floor of the composition, and so the number the stacking threshold is set from.
	   14, not 13.5. Raising the floor in UNITS raises it in pixels at every width — it is the other
	   half of `R = NATIVE / floor`, and the half that does not cost the drawing anything as long as
	   the words still fit. Measured that they do: the longest note here is "Act with equipment
	   context" at 171 units, which becomes 177 at 14, and the card's text column is 180 because
	   "Gateways and platforms" needs that on one line. 14.5 would have put two of the six notes over
	   it, and a note that wraps puts the three items out of step with the card opposite. */
	.plat__item-note {
		margin: u(3) 0 0;
		font-size: u(14);
		line-height: 1.35;
		color: var(--plat-muted);
	}

	// --- the platform, raised ------------------------------------------------------------------

	// No border. A hairline as well as a shadow reads as a card with an outline drawn on it; the
	// shadow on its own is what lifts it off the two zones. The recipe is the page's — a 1px contact
	// shadow that does not scale, over an ambient one that does.
	.plat__core {
		position: relative;
		// Over the side columns, not between them: the gutter track is gone (COL.gut is 0) and the
		// card pays for the overlap itself. `z-index` because it has to paint ON the zone cards —
		// its shadow falling across their inner edge is the reading that makes it the raised object.
		z-index: 1;
		margin-inline: calc(-1 * #{u(var(--plat-overlap))});
		grid-column: 3;
		grid-row: 3;
		// Top padding is the badge's lower half plus the air under it, so the heading clears a badge
		// that is half outside the card: BADGE / 2 + 12.
		padding: calc(u(var(--plat-badge)) / 2 + #{u(12)}) u(20) u(24);
		border-radius: u(12);
		background: var(--plat-surface);
		// Overridable through `--flow-shadow`, as the other visuals in this family are, so a token set
		// on the row can retune every one of them at once instead of this being the one that ignores it.
		box-shadow: var(
			--flow-shadow,
			0 1px 2px rgba(16, 24, 40, 0.04),
			0 #{u(12)} #{u(28)} #{u(-8)} rgba(16, 24, 40, 0.12)
		);
	}

	// The connect section's badge, placed the way the ecosystem cards place theirs: centred on the
	// card's TOP EDGE, half above it and half below. The page's shared 32% squircle, the mark in
	// white on the brand, and a shadow that lifts the tile off the card it overlaps.
	//
	// The shadow is the unit-scaled one the connect visual uses, not the ecosystem card's fixed
	// pixels — that card is not built on a design unit, this is, and a px shadow would be the same
	// size at every scale.
	.plat__core-badge {
		position: absolute;
		top: 0;
		left: 50%;
		transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		width: u(var(--plat-badge));
		height: u(var(--plat-badge));
		padding: u(var(--plat-badge-pad));
		border-radius: 32%;
		color: var(--color-white, #fff);
		// Black rather than the brand: the badge was the third indigo object in a drawing that now
		// carries indigo on the right-hand runs and their labels, and the mark is the one thing here
		// that is the product itself rather than a part of it. The gradient is slight on purpose —
		// enough that a 76-unit tile does not read as a flat black sticker, not so much that it
		// becomes a button.
		background: linear-gradient(155deg, #2c2f37 0%, #111318 100%);
		box-shadow: var(
			--flow-shadow-badge,
			0 1px 2px rgba(16, 24, 40, 0.06),
			0 #{u(7)} #{u(16)} #{u(-5)} rgba(16, 24, 40, 0.22)
		);
	}

	// Smaller, because the badge above it is now carrying the identity: the heading only has to name
	// what the badge already showed.
	// Ink, not the brand. With the four stages now coded to the four sections, the brand indigo is
	// spoken for — it is the solution section's, which Operate carries — and a heading in it read as
	// a fifth code rather than as a title. The badge holds the brand on its own.
	.plat__core-title {
		margin: 0 0 u(16);
		text-align: center;
		font-size: u(22);
		font-weight: $font-weight-bold;
		line-height: 1.1;
		letter-spacing: -0.025em;
		color: var(--plat-ink);
	}

	// Four across, at every width the wide cut is used at. Two rows of two was tried and taken out
	// again: it buys about 2.6px of floor at a 960 column, and it costs the thing the drawing is
	// actually saying — a left-to-right loop with four steps in the middle of it. Stacked two by two
	// the middle reads as a block of four tiles rather than as a run. The stacked cut still uses two
	// by two, because at 480 units there is no run left to preserve.
	.plat__stages {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
		column-gap: u(12);
		margin: 0;
		// The rule under the heading, carrying the four stages' own accents left to right — each
		// colour sits over the stage it belongs to, so the line is a key to the row under it rather
		// than a divider that happens to be coloured.
		//
		// A background rather than a border: a border cannot take a gradient, and at one pixel there
		// was nothing for four colours to happen in. Three units gives them room. It paints in the
		// top of the padding box, so the gap between it and the icons is unchanged.
		padding: u(16) 0 0;
		background-image: linear-gradient(
			90deg,
			var(--plat-stage-1) 12.5%,
			var(--plat-stage-2) 37.5%,
			var(--plat-stage-3) 62.5%,
			var(--plat-stage-4) 87.5%
		);
		background-repeat: no-repeat;
		background-size: 100% #{u(3)};
		border-radius: u(1.5);
		list-style: none;
	}

	// In flow, not absolute: the rail it used to have to dodge is gone.
	// On a squircle, as the ecosystem cards and the app badge above set theirs — 32% radius, the
	// glyph in its stage's hue over a wash of the same. 26 in a 44-unit tile: the stage column is
	// about 113 units wide, so the tile can take that much without crowding the verb under it.
	.plat__stage-icon {
		display: block;
		box-sizing: content-box;
		width: u(26);
		height: u(26);
		padding: u(9);
		margin-bottom: u(12);
		border-radius: 32%;
		// The only colour left in the middle column, and the thing that tells the four apart.
		color: var(--stage);
		background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--stage) 10%, #fff);
	}

	// Ink, not indigo. The heading, the mark and the icons are the column's colour; the four verbs
	// are its content, and colouring them made all four things compete for the same attention.
	.plat__stage-name {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: u(20);
		font-weight: $font-weight-bold;
		line-height: 1.15;
		letter-spacing: -0.025em;
		color: var(--plat-ink);
	}

	.plat__stage-note {
		margin: u(6) 0 0;
		font-size: u(14);
		line-height: 1.35;
		color: var(--plat-muted);
	}

	.plat__found {
		display: none;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
		column-gap: u(18);
		margin: u(22) 0 0;
		padding: u(18) 0 0;
		border-top: max(1px, #{u(1)}) solid var(--plat-line);
		list-style: none;
	}

	.plat[data-found] .plat__found {
		display: grid;
	}

	.plat__found-item {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: u(9);
		font-size: u(14);
		font-weight: $font-weight-medium;
		letter-spacing: -0.01em;
		color: var(--plat-platform);
	}

	.plat__found-icon {
		flex: none;
		width: u(21);
		height: u(21);
	}

	// --- the four runs ------------------------------------------------------------------------

	.plat__band {
		// The whole composition, not the middle three tracks: a run now starts over its side card.
		grid-column: 1 / -1;
	}

	.plat__band--in {
		grid-row: 2;
	}

	.plat__band--out {
		grid-row: 4;
	}

	.plat__wire[data-zone='equipment'],
	.plat__head[data-zone='equipment'] {
		stroke: var(--plat-wire-in);
	}

	.plat__wire[data-zone='people'],
	.plat__head[data-zone='people'] {
		stroke: var(--plat-wire-out);
	}

	// The labels follow their own run. Leaving them grey while the stroke under them took a hue read
	// as an unfinished job rather than as a decision.
	.plat__run[data-zone='equipment'] {
		color: var(--plat-wire-in);
	}

	.plat__run[data-zone='people'] {
		color: var(--plat-wire-out);
	}

	// The CSS halves of the U's, which are not in the SVG and so cannot be reached by `data-zone`.
	.plat__col--equipment {
		--plat-wire: var(--plat-wire-in);
	}

	.plat__col--people {
		--plat-wire: var(--plat-wire-out);
	}

	// The wire's own weight, not a heavier one: a thicker pip reads as a bulge travelling down the
	// line, where at the same weight it is the line itself lighting up and only colour changes.
	// White, by `ConnectHub`'s own formula — the wire's accent mixed to 28%, which lands close enough
	// to white to read as light while keeping a whisper of the route's colour.
	.plat__pip {
		fill: none;
		stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--plat-wire) 28%, #fff);
		stroke-width: 3;
		stroke-linecap: round;
		stroke-dasharray: var(--plat-pip-dash);
		// Negative, so every wire is already part-way through its cycle on the first frame rather
		// than holding dark until its turn comes round.
		animation: plat-pip var(--plat-cycle) linear infinite;
		animation-delay: calc(-1 * var(--pip-phase, 0ms));
	}

	.plat__pip[data-zone='equipment'] {
		--plat-wire: var(--plat-wire-in);
	}

	.plat__pip[data-zone='people'] {
		--plat-wire: var(--plat-wire-out);
	}

	// One full pattern per cycle: start and end are the same picture, so the loop has no seam.
	@keyframes plat-pip {
		from {
			stroke-dashoffset: var(--plat-pip-pattern);
		}

		to {
			stroke-dashoffset: 0;
		}
	}

	// The only thing in this drawing that moves for its own sake. The picture is complete without
	// it — the arrowheads already carry the direction, and the signal is emphasis on top of them.
	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
		.plat__pip {
			animation: none;
		}
	}

	// The global reset gives every `svg` a `max-width: 100%`; stated away rather than relied on.
	.plat__wires {
		display: block;
		// An `svg` clips to its viewBox, and every arrowhead in the band has its POINT on that edge —
		// a 3-unit stroke with a round join paints 1.5 further out than the geometry it follows, so
		// each point was being sliced flat. `HEAD_BOX_H` solves this for the stems by padding their
		// box; the band cannot, because its box is the row. So it lets the overhang paint.
		overflow: visible;
		width: 100%;
		max-width: none;
		height: 100%;
	}

	.plat__wire,
	.plat__head {
		fill: none;
		stroke: var(--plat-wire);
		stroke-width: 3;
		stroke-linecap: round;
		stroke-linejoin: round;
	}

	// The box IS the run's horizontal leg — same number the path is drawn from — so centring the text
	// inside it centres the text on the arrow, at every width and whatever the label says.
	// Set in caps, which is what separates a label on a line from the prose in the cards either side
	// of it without needing a second colour or a rule.
	//
	// 13.5, NOT smaller, even though a caps label usually is. 13.5 is the composition's floor — the
	// notes under each item and each stage — and dropping the labels below it would raise R above 86
	// and make the phone worse for every other element too. Caps at the floor still read as a label
	// because of the tracking; caps below it would just be the new floor.
	// NOWRAP, and the box is allowed to be too small for the text.
	//
	// The box is still exactly the run's leg, so the text still centres on the arrow — that part has
	// to stay. But the leg is derived from the middle column, and the middle went from 560 units to
	// 310 when the stages became two rows of two, which took the leg from 212 to 79. "Views and
	// alerts" needs 143 units set in caps, so it broke over three lines and read as a paragraph
	// floating above an arrow.
	//
	// Overflowing a centred box is symmetric, so the text keeps its centre and simply runs wider than
	// its leg. Nothing is in its way: the labels are in grid rows 1 and 5 and the cards are in row 3,
	// so the only thing they could meet is each other — and measured at the narrowest container the
	// wide cut is used at, the two top labels are 189 units apart.
	.plat__run {
		width: u(var(--plat-leg));
		margin: 0;
		white-space: nowrap;
		font-size: u(14);
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.085em;
		text-align: center;
		font-weight: $font-weight-medium;
		line-height: 1.3;
		color: var(--plat-wire);
	}

	// Each label is a box exactly as wide as its run's horizontal reach, offset to where that reach
	// starts — so centring the text inside it centres the text on the arrow, and a label made longer
	// or shorter later stays centred because the box never moves.
	.plat__run {
		grid-column: 1 / -1;
		justify-self: start;
		width: u(var(--plat-run-w));
	}

	.plat__run--telemetry,
	.plat__run--commands {
		margin-left: u(var(--plat-attach));
	}

	.plat__run--views,
	.plat__run--actions {
		margin-left: u(var(--plat-core-out));
	}

	// 4, not 10. A label belongs to its line, and at ten units of air it read as a caption floating
	// above the band rather than as the name of the arrow under it.
	.plat__run--telemetry,
	.plat__run--views {
		grid-row: 1;
		margin-bottom: u(4);
	}

	.plat__run--commands,
	.plat__run--actions {
		grid-row: 5;
		margin-top: u(4);
	}

	.plat__stem {
		display: none;
	}

	.plat__stem-line {
		flex: 1 1 auto;
		width: u(3);
		border-radius: u(1.5);
		background: currentColor;
	}

	.plat__stem-head {
		flex: none;
		width: u(14);
		// The box, not the path: see `HEAD_BOX_H`. The line is `flex: 1` and absorbs the difference,
		// so the stem's total height is unchanged and the point still meets the card.
		height: u(var(--plat-head-h));
		max-width: none;
		margin-top: u(-6);
		fill: none;
		stroke: currentColor;
		stroke-width: 3;
		stroke-linecap: round;
		stroke-linejoin: round;
	}

	// --- stacked -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	//
	// 768, not `PlatformLoop`'s 1000. The drawing is meant to stay horizontal and simply scale, and
	// to go vertical for phones and small tablets — so the number is the one the page around it
	// already switches at: `_home-rows.scss` stacks the homepage's rows at 768 too, and a visual that
	// turned at a different width from its own row would be two layout changes on one scroll.
	//
	// What it costs, stated rather than discovered later: at 768 the floor is 9.8px, and the wide cut
	// is asked to hold down to there. Below it the picture stacks and re-derives its unit from 480
	// units, which puts R at 34 and the floor back at 10.9px on a 367px phone column.
	@container (max-width: 768px) {
		.plat {
			--u: var(--plat-u-narrow);
			grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
			// 48, not 52. `minmax` rather than a fixed height so a label that needs a second line takes
			// it rather than overflowing its row — "Views and alerts" does, at this width.
			grid-template-rows: auto minmax(u(48), auto) auto minmax(u(48), auto) auto;
			max-width: u(var(--plat-narrow));
			margin-inline: auto;
			row-gap: 0;
		}

		// The two outer cards are narrower than the core, and centred. 340 is the width the CORE
		// needs; the zones need 245 — "Gateways and platforms" at 180 plus 65 of chrome — so at the
